\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Lemma}
\title{The matrix algebra in even dimension}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{For #{2m} positive complex Clifford directions, #{m\ge0},
the algebra generated subject only to the Clifford relations of
\ref{fdip-00LZ} is the full matrix algebra on
#{S_0=(\mathbb C^2)^{\otimes m}}. Every unital finite-dimensional
Hermitian module with self-adjoint directions is unitarily
equivalent to #{S_0\otimes E}, with the algebra acting on the
first factor. The simple module is #{S_0} up to equivalence.}

\proof{
\p{Use the orthonormal tensor basis of \ref{fdip-00IO}. Let
#{X} exchange its two single-factor basis vectors, let
#{Z=\operatorname{diag}(1,-1)}, and take #{Y=\ui XZ}.
Thus #{Y} sends the first vector to #{\ui} times the second
and the second to #{-\ui} times the first. For #{1\le r\le m},
the operators in positions #{2r-1,2r} are
##{\begin{aligned}
\varepsilon_{2r-1}&=Z^{\otimes(r-1)}\otimes X\otimes\bbI^{\otimes(m-r)},\\
\varepsilon_{2r}&=Z^{\otimes(r-1)}\otimes Y\otimes\bbI^{\otimes(m-r)} .
\end{aligned}}
Each is a self-adjoint involution. At a common active position
#{X} and #{Y} anticommute. At different active positions the
earlier #{X} or #{Y} crosses the later string's #{Z} exactly
once, and all other factors commute. These arrays represent
the relations.}

\p{In the first factor the generated algebra contains #{X,Y}
and #{Z=-\ui XY}. Inductively the earlier single-factor
#{Z}'s cancel the string at position #{r}, recovering #{X,Y,Z}
on that factor alone. The projections #{(\bbI\pm Z)/2} and
their products with #{X} give all single-factor matrix units.
Their tensor products give the matrix units for #{S_0}.
The image is therefore the full matrix algebra, of dimension
#{(2^m)^2}. Reordering and cancelling repeated generators spans
the abstract algebra by at most #{2^{2m}} increasing words.
The dimensions force the representation to be an isomorphism.
At #{m=0} the algebra and the tensor model are both scalars.}

\p{Write #{e_{ij}} for these matrix units and #{E=e_{11}S}
for the first diagonal subspace of a module #{S}. In this
realization #{e_{ij}^\dagger=e_{ji}}, and the represented
units retain this identity because the generators are
self-adjoint. The #{e_{ii}} are orthogonal projections summing
to the identity. The map #{e_{i1}} carries #{E} isometrically
onto #{e_{ii}S}, with inverse #{e_{1i}} on that range.
Taking the #{i}th basis vector of #{S_0} tensored with #{v}
to #{e_{i1}v} is the desired unitary intertwiner.
The full matrix action on #{S_0} is irreducible, and splitting
#{E} in an orthonormal basis writes every module as its copies.}
}
